SRTP: AEAD_AES_256_GCM only (no AES_CM_128/SHA1 fallback anywhere), cryptex
negotiated by default, and close the disableEncryption plaintext-RTP escape
hatch (finding #12).

Q-Audion 1:1 calls must never fall back to a non-AEAD or 128-bit SRTP cipher,
however CryptoOptions happens to be constructed by a caller:
- CryptoOptions::GetSupportedDtlsSrtpCryptoSuites() (api/crypto/crypto_options.cc)
  returns exactly {kSrtpAeadAes256Gcm} in strict (rtc_qaudion_transport_strict=true,
  QAUDION_TRANSPORT_STRICT=1) builds, ignoring every srtp.* toggle
  (enable_aes128_sha1_32/80_crypto_cipher, prefer_gcm_crypto_suites, ...): AES_CM_128_HMAC_SHA1_32/80
  and AEAD_AES_128_GCM are compiled out of the offer entirely, not merely
  de-preferred. Every peer we call is our own M150 build with GCM enabled by
  default (since M144), so the intersection with the remote offer is never
  empty (RTC_CHECK(!crypto_suites.empty()) in the untouched upstream body
  below, which strict builds never reach).
  In switchable (LiveKit) builds the upstream body is untouched at transport
  level < 1; at level >= 1 (raised at runtime via the tighten-only
  qaudion::RaiseTransportLevel(), P8, once the room's SFU is confirmed to
  relay AEAD_AES_256_GCM) it also collapses to {kSrtpAeadAes256Gcm}.
- CryptoOptions::Srtp::cryptex_policy (api/crypto/crypto_options.h) defaults
  to kNegotiate in strict builds instead of upstream's kDisabled (still a
  TODO upstream: bugs.webrtc.org/455813732). Every peer is our own M150
  build, so cryptex is always successfully negotiated; this does not by
  itself require AES-256 (GetSupportedDtlsSrtpCryptoSuites() and P7 already
  do that) - it only stops RTP header extensions from being the one part of
  the packet cryptex would otherwise leave in the clear. Switchable builds
  keep the upstream default unchanged.

Finding #12 (disableEncryption, MEDIUM): `PeerConnectionFactory.Options().
disableEncryption` (Android/iOS) sets JsepTransportController::Config::
disable_encryption, which CreateRtpTransport() (pc/jsep_transport_controller.cc)
used unconditionally to build a CreateUnencryptedRtpTransport() instead of a
DtlsSrtpTransport() - i.e. it skips DTLS-SRTP altogether and hands raw,
plaintext RTP/RTCP to the network. Every other AES-256 guarantee in this
series (P1 FrameCryptor, this patch's SRTP cipher list, P7's DTLS policy) is
moot if that single flag is left reachable, since it removes SRTP entirely
rather than downgrading its cipher.
  Fix: the local `disable_encryption` actually consumed by CreateRtpTransport()
  is forced to `false` in strict builds regardless of what the app passed in
  Options(). In switchable (LiveKit) builds it is honored only below
  transport level 1 - i.e. it stops working the moment the app raises the
  level (tighten-only), matching "force it false when the app has raised the
  transport level" from the review.

Depends on Q-Audion's own runtime API (rtc_base/qaudion_tuning.h, written by
P8, applied earlier in the series - see m150/series) for:
- qaudion::TransportLevel() - already named in the P8 sketch (plan section
  2.3);
- the QAUDION_TRANSPORT_STRICT build-wide #define (also P8, webrtc.gni +
  root BUILD.gn's common_inherited_config).
No new qaudion:: names are introduced by this patch.

Order: sixth in the series (P1, P2, P3, P8, P4a, P4b, P5, P6, P7 - see
m150/series, owned by the audio/API author). Depends on P8 for the
qaudion_tuning.h header and the QAUDION_TRANSPORT_STRICT define; applied
after P4/P5 (neither of which touches these files) per the fixed patch order.

